          "text": "Over the last 3 weeks you averaged about **7 hours a night**, but the past few nights have been shorter. There's also a problem with the data: 20 of the 21 nights look unreliable.\n\n**How long you slept (Feb 26 – Mar 18)**\n\n| Week | Average sleep |\n|---|---|\n| Feb 26 – Mar 4 | ~7.2 h |\n| Mar 5 – Mar 11 | ~7.0 h |\n| Mar 12 – Mar 18 | ~6.7 h |\n\n- **Longest nights:** Mar 3 (8.1 h) and Mar 10 (8.0 h)\n- **Under 6 hours:** Mar 7 (5.9 h) and Mar 17 (5.7 h)\n- **Last 3 nights:** 6.2 h, 5.7 h and 6.25 h, your shortest run of the period\n\n**Last night (Mar 18) looks reliable**\n- Sleep score 74 (\"Fair\"), your best of the period\n- 6.25 h asleep, roughly 23:10 to 05:50, with one wake-up of about 26 minutes\n- Sleep stages: 18.7% deep, 21.3% REM, 60% light\n- Average overnight HRV 58 ms, sleep stress 14, average SpO₂ 95%\n\n**The other 20 nights (Feb 26 – Mar 17) probably don't reflect how you actually slept**\n- **The numbers are identical every night:** sleep score 63, HRV 61 ms, sleep stress 19, SpO₂ 94% (lowest 87%), and exactly 2 wake-ups totalling 13 minutes. The share of deep, light and REM sleep barely changes either, even though total sleep varied from 5.7 to 8.1 hours. Real sleep doesn't repeat like that.\n- **The times don't add up:** every night shows the same bedtime and wake time (about 23:45 to 05:40, under 6 hours in bed). Yet most nights report more than 6 hours of sleep.\n\nSo the trend in total sleep may hold, but I can't give you a reliable picture of your sleep quality before last night. This could be a sync problem, nights without the watch, or data filled in by another app. It's worth opening a few of those nights in Garmin Connect to see whether they show real overnight graphs.\n\n**Bottom line:** going by total sleep, you've been close to 7 hours on average but slipping to around 6 hours this week. If that matches how you feel, getting back to 7+ hours would be the main thing to work on.\n\nTraining data only, not medical advice.",
